# ORIN-EL1AP (baton orin-8 item 1, SMP-redesign Candidate C): UNAOS_ORINEL1AP=1 arms `orinel1ap` —
# drop exactly ONE PSCI-woken AP from EL2 to EL1 during `smp_virt` bring-up, so `el1cores` gains a
# second bit and `bg` has a core to host a background EL0 tenant on.
#
# TODAY, UNARMED: only the BSP is at EL1 and it runs the shell, so every `bg` answers
#   `BGRUN: rejected (no core is at EL1 to host a background EL0 task)`
# two lines above `rollup: el0refuse=<n> el1cores=0x1`. The refusal is CORRECT and hardware-proven —
# an `eret` on an EL2 AP banks ELR_EL2 and kills the core — so this does NOT touch the filter. It
# supplies the thing the filter was always waiting for: a core that has MEASURED itself at EL1.
#
# WHAT ARMING CHANGES: one AP (whichever secondary reaches the claim first — the witness names it)
# waits for the BSP's own JM6 drop to publish its EL1 root, then replays that same drop
# (`enable_el1_regime` + `drop_el2_to_el1_tegra`, reused verbatim — every register they write is
# per-core banked) on itself, re-seeds TPIDR_EL1/VBAR_EL1, stamps `EL1_CORE_MASK`, unmasks IRQ and
# enters `sched::secondary_run` at EL1. Every OTHER AP is untouched and stays an EL2 scheduler
# participant, so SPREAD balance for ordinary kernel tasks does not move.
#
# READ IT ON THE WIRE — every line carries `[el1ap]`, refusals included (silence must never be
# indistinguishable from "never ran"):
#     LC_ALL=C grep -a -o '\[el1ap\][^:]*' <log>
#   `[el1ap] seat claimed by cpu=N — waiting up to 30 s for the BSP EL1 root`
#   `[el1ap] cpu=N dropping EL2 -> EL1 (TTBR0_EL1=0x... , the BSP-installed L1_EL1)`
#   `[el1ap] cpu=N LANDED at EL1 — stamped=true el1cores=0x... irq=unmasked (EL0-EL1CORE)`
#   `[el1ap] REFUSED cpu=N — ...` (wrong EL / seat held / root not published / post-eret not EL1)
# The corroborating line is sched.rs's own `[el0core] el1 core MEASURED: cpu=N mask=0x...`, and the
# verdict is the rollup: `el1cores=` must show TWO bits, and `bg` must stop answering el0refuse.
#
# `orinel1ap = ["tegrasmp", "tegra_el0"]` (both imply `tegra`), so the knob is self-sufficient:
# `tegrasmp` because the AP must exist to be dropped (`start_secondaries_tegra` is itself
# `#[cfg(feature = "tegrasmp")]`), `tegra_el0` because an EL1 AP exists to host an EL0 tenant and the
# spawn surface is gated `any(baremetal, tegra_el0)` — `baremetal` implies `pi`, and `pi` + `tegra` is
# a hard `compile_error!`, so `tegra_el0` is the only satisfiable term on this board. DEFAULT OFF =>
# the block, the publishing statement, the call site and the accessor all vanish. The ARMED polarity
# is type-checked by the `arm-tegra-el1ap` leg of KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX, never by this mapping.
#
# NOT PROVEN ON HARDWARE. Nothing in this arc has been on the bench.
[ -n "${UNAOS_ORINEL1AP:-}" ] && _feats="${_feats}orinel1ap,tegrasmp,tegra_el0,tegra,"
