# ── GATE-CFG-MIX: the endpoints are not the configurations we ship ────────────────────────────────
#
# THE BLIND SPOT this closes. KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX above and the two DEFAULT legs in check_both between
# them exercise exactly two points of the x86 feature cube: everything OFF (the default legs) and
# everything ON (`x86-all`). Every configuration anyone actually boots is in the MIDDLE, and the
# middle was never compiled. That is not theoretical either. The igpu lane's Flight 1 shipped a stray
# `#[cfg(all(target_arch = "x86_64", feature = "gmux_igd"))]` that bound to `pub fn init` in
# `drivers/gpu/igpu.rs`, gating the entire iGPU probe entry point behind `gmux_igd`. The call site,
# `arch/x86_64/pci.rs`, is gated on `intel-ivb`. So:
#   * all features OFF  -> neither the definition nor the call site is compiled. GREEN.
#   * all features ON   -> both are compiled, the call resolves. GREEN.
#   * intel-ivb ON, gmux_igd OFF (the NORMAL rMBP build, and what the bench media carries)
#                       -> E0425 `cannot find function 'init'`. RED — and nothing ran it.
# Both required gates passed a branch that could not build the machine it was written for.
#
# THE DEFECT CLASS, stated precisely: a symbol that exists only under feature X is referenced from
# code that is compiled under feature Y (or under no gate at all). Detecting it needs one compile in
# which Y is ON and X is OFF. The ungated case (Y = nothing) is already covered — the DEFAULT legs
# compile the all-off state. What was missing is the feature-vs-feature case.
#
# THE LEG-SELECTION RULE (why this many legs and not more). The full powerset is 2^N and absurd.
# Per-feature sweeps (each ON alone; each dropped from the full set) are linear — 2N legs, which at
# today's N = 75 is 150 (~220 s was measured back when N was 39, so budget well over that today)
# — which is too slow to sit in `check` on every edit. But complete coverage of the defect
# class does not need N legs at all, it needs an ORDERED-PAIR covering array, and that is LOGARITHMIC:
#
#   Give feature i a distinct n-bit mask M_i, all masks of the SAME popcount n/2 (an antichain), and
#   define leg j = { feature i : bit j of M_i is set }. For any two distinct features X != Y, M_X and
#   M_Y are distinct sets of equal size, so neither contains the other, so some bit j is set in M_Y
#   and clear in M_X. Leg j therefore has Y ON and X OFF. Every ordered pair is covered, by
#   construction, with n legs — and n is the smallest integer with C(n, n/2) >= N, i.e. the PROVABLY
#   MINIMAL number of legs for which such a family of masks exists at all. For today's N = 75 x86
#   features that is n = 9, because C(8,4) = 70 < 75 <= C(9,4) = 126. Verified empirically at
#   authoring time, when N was 39 and n was 8: 0 uncovered ordered pairs of 1482.
#
#   THE LEG COUNT IS DERIVED, NOT CONFIGURED: build_cfg_legs searches for that smallest n on every
#   run, so it moves on its own as the feature table grows. Every count written in prose below is
#   therefore a re-count hazard — read `${#KERNEL_CFG_MIX[@]}` on the gate's own summary line for the
#   live number, and re-count these comments when the universe crosses a C(n, n/2) boundary.
#
# So the default gate pays 9 compiles (~23 s cold was measured when it was 8) for complete pairwise
# cfg coverage of the x86 feature cube. The `gmux_igd`/`intel-ivb` pair is covered several times over.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER, stated honestly. Strength 2. A symbol under X referenced from code gated
# on `all(feature = "Y", feature = "Z")` needs a leg with Y and Z on and X off; the covering array
# guarantees that only for pairs, not triples. The `x86-drop-<feat>` sweep legs behind
# UNAOS_CFG_SWEEP=1 close exactly that direction — each drops ONE feature with all N-1 others ON, so
# every conjunction of other features is compiled — at the cost of N more compiles.
#
# UNAOS_CFG_SWEEP=1 adds, on top of the 9 default mix legs:
#   * `x86-only-<feat>`  — that feature ALONE (N legs, 75 today). Answers "does this feature stand up
#                          by itself", and localizes: the mix legs prove a defect EXISTS but name a
#                          leg carrying ~35 features; this names one.
#   * `x86-drop-<feat>`  — the full x86 set MINUS that feature (N legs, 75 today). The
#                          strength-unbounded direction described above.
# Measured on the clean tree at authoring time, when N was 39: on-alone 39/39 green, leave-one-out
# 39/39 green, ~220 s for the pair.
#
# THE FEATURE LIST IS DERIVED, NOT COPIED. A second hardcoded list would drift out of `[features]` —
# and already had: `nvidia-kepler-kdisp-hold` is declared in crates/kernel/Cargo.toml and appears in
# NO leg of KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX, so it had never been type-checked at all. So the universe is computed
# every run as
#       (every feature in crates/kernel/Cargo.toml's [features] table, minus `default`)
#     - (the aarch64-only features, = what the arm-* legs above name and `x86-all` does not)
# which makes Cargo.toml the single source of truth and puts a NEW feature under coverage the moment
# it is declared. Corollary, and the rule for anyone adding a knob: a new feature is treated as x86
# unless it is named on an arm-* leg. Declare an aarch64-only feature without adding it to arm-pi or
# arm-tegra and these legs will try to compile it on x86 and the gate will go red naming it — which
# is the intended forcing function, not a bug.

# Emit the kernel's feature names, one per line, `default` excluded — read straight out of the
# `[features]` table. Every entry in that table is a single `name = [...]` line; the guard in
# x86_cfg_universe fails loudly if this parse ever returns an implausible count.
kernel_feature_table() {
    awk '
        /^\[features\]/ { inf = 1; next }
        /^\[/           { inf = 0 }
        inf && /^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+[ \t]*=[ \t]*\[/ {
            sub(/[ \t]*=.*/, "", $0)
            if ($0 != "default") print $0
        }
    ' "$WORKSPACE_DIR/crates/kernel/Cargo.toml"
}

# The x86 sweep universe: the whole feature table minus the features the matrix marks aarch64-only.
# Prints one feature per line. Returns 1 (and says why) if the Cargo.toml parse looks broken, so a
# silently-empty universe can never masquerade as a green gate.
x86_cfg_universe() {
    local _all _armonly _x86all _f _n _row _leg _tgt _feats
    _all="$(kernel_feature_table)"
    _n=$(printf '%s\n' "$_all" | grep -c . || true)
    if [ "$_n" -lt 40 ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}❌ kernel cfg coverage: parsed only ${_n} features from crates/kernel/Cargo.toml — [features] parse is broken${COLOR_RESET}" >&2
        return 1
    fi
    # arm-only = named by an arm-* leg and NOT by x86-all.
    _x86all=""; _armonly=""
    for _row in "${KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX[@]}"; do
        read -r _leg _tgt _feats <<< "$_row"
        case "$_leg" in
            x86-*) _x86all="${_x86all},${_feats}" ;;
            arm-*) _armonly="${_armonly},${_feats}" ;;
        esac
    done
    # `|| true` on every grep: a filter that removes the last line returns 1, and `set -e` would turn
    # that into a silent abort of the whole gate instead of a verdict.
    for _f in $(printf '%s\n' "$_armonly" | tr ',' '\n' | grep -v '^$' | sort -u); do
        case ",${_x86all}," in *",${_f},"*) ;; *) _all="$(printf '%s\n' "$_all" | grep -vx "$_f" || true)" ;; esac
    done
    printf '%s\n' "$_all" | grep -v '^$' || true
}

# Fill KERNEL_CFG_MIX (always) and KERNEL_CFG_SWEEP (only under UNAOS_CFG_SWEEP=1) with rows in the
# exact 3-field shape KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX uses: "label  target-json  comma,separated,features".
KERNEL_CFG_MIX=()
KERNEL_CFG_SWEEP=()
build_cfg_legs() {
    local _uni _N _n _c _m _bit _feats _i _f _full
    local -a _U=() _MASK=()
    _uni="$(x86_cfg_universe)" || return 1
    while read -r _f; do [ -n "$_f" ] && _U+=("$_f"); done <<< "$_uni"
    _N=${#_U[@]}

    # Smallest n with C(n, n/2) >= N, found by counting the popcount-n/2 masks directly (n <= 10 here,
    # so this is a few hundred iterations of integer arithmetic — free next to one cargo invocation).
    _n=1
    while :; do
        _c=0
        for ((_m = 0; _m < (1 << _n); _m++)); do
            _bit=$_m; _i=0
            while [ "$_bit" -ne 0 ]; do _i=$((_i + (_bit & 1))); _bit=$((_bit >> 1)); done
            [ "$_i" -eq $((_n / 2)) ] && _c=$((_c + 1))
        done
        [ "$_c" -ge "$_N" ] && break
        _n=$((_n + 1))
    done
    # The masks themselves, in increasing integer order, first N of them.
    for ((_m = 0; _m < (1 << _n) && ${#_MASK[@]} < _N; _m++)); do
        _bit=$_m; _i=0
        while [ "$_bit" -ne 0 ]; do _i=$((_i + (_bit & 1))); _bit=$((_bit >> 1)); done
        [ "$_i" -eq $((_n / 2)) ] && _MASK+=("$_m")
    done

    for ((_bit = 0; _bit < _n; _bit++)); do
        _feats=""
        for ((_i = 0; _i < _N; _i++)); do
            (( (_MASK[_i] >> _bit) & 1 )) && _feats="${_feats},${_U[_i]}"
        done
        KERNEL_CFG_MIX+=("x86-mix-${_bit} ../../x86_64-unaos.json ${_feats#,}")
    done

    [ "${UNAOS_CFG_SWEEP:-0}" = "1" ] || return 0
    _full="$(printf '%s,' "${_U[@]}")"; _full="${_full%,}"
    for ((_i = 0; _i < _N; _i++)); do
        KERNEL_CFG_SWEEP+=("x86-only-${_U[_i]} ../../x86_64-unaos.json ${_U[_i]}")
    done
    for ((_i = 0; _i < _N; _i++)); do
        _feats="$(printf '%s\n' "$_full" | tr ',' '\n' | { grep -vx "${_U[_i]}" || true; } | tr '\n' ',')"
        KERNEL_CFG_SWEEP+=("x86-drop-${_U[_i]} ../../x86_64-unaos.json ${_feats%,}")
    done
}

# Type-check every KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX leg and print one summary line per leg, in the same shape as the
# kernel and userspace legs above.
#
# Like check_user_arch it does NOT stop at the first failure: a broken shared helper typically breaks
# more than one leg, and aborting on the first would hand back one leg per run — a full round-trip
# each. Every failing leg is collected and NAMED (leg label + the crate that failed to compile), and
# the function returns 1, which check_both turns into a non-zero exit for the whole gate.
#
# NOTE this deliberately does NOT consult $KERNEL_FEATURES. These legs are a FIXED coverage matrix:
# if an operator's UNAOS_* environment could add to them, the gate would mean something different for
# every operator, and "check green" would stop being a shared fact. The operator's own knob set is
# already what the two default legs compile.
#
# The board matrix runs FIRST and the derived GATE-CFG-MIX legs behind it: the board legs are the ones
# an edit is most likely to break, so their diagnostics stay at the top of the scrollback. A red mix
# leg names the leg AND its exact `--features` string, because the label alone ("x86-mix-3") does not
# tell you which configuration broke and the whole point of these legs is to name the configuration.
check_kernel_cfg() {
    local _rc=0 _n=0 _failed=""
    local _row _leg _tgt _feats
    local -a _rows=()
    build_cfg_legs || return 1
    _rows=("${KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX[@]}" "${KERNEL_CFG_MIX[@]}" "${KERNEL_CFG_SWEEP[@]}")
    echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚡ Syntax Check: kernel cfg-gated coverage (${#_rows[@]} legs)...${COLOR_RESET}"
    if [ "${UNAOS_CFG_SWEEP:-0}" != "1" ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_CYAN}  (${#KERNEL_CFG_MATRIX[@]} board + ${#KERNEL_CFG_MIX[@]} x86 pairwise-mix; UNAOS_CFG_SWEEP=1 adds the per-feature sweep)${COLOR_RESET}"
    fi
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR/crates/kernel"
    for _row in "${_rows[@]}"; do
        read -r _leg _tgt _feats <<< "$_row"
        _n=$((_n + 1))
        echo -e "${COLOR_CYAN}  → ${_leg}${COLOR_RESET}"
        # `if ... else` (not a bare command) so `set -e` cannot abort the loop before every leg has run.
        if cargo +nightly check --release --target "$_tgt" $NIGHTLY_FLAGS --features "$_feats"; then
            echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}  ✅ ${_leg}${COLOR_RESET}"
        else
            echo -e "${COLOR_RED}  ❌ ${_leg} — unaos-kernel FAILED to compile${COLOR_RESET}"
            # Name the CONFIGURATION, not just the leg. For the board legs the label already implies
            # it; for a derived mix/sweep leg the feature string IS the finding — it is the thing the
            # author has to reproduce, and reconstructing it from a label costs a round-trip.
            echo -e "${COLOR_RED}     configuration: --target ${_tgt} --features ${_feats}${COLOR_RESET}"
            _failed="${_failed} ${_leg}"
            _rc=1
        fi
    done
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR"
    if [ "$_rc" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}❌ kernel cfg coverage FAILED (crate unaos-kernel), legs:${_failed}${COLOR_RESET}"
        return 1
    fi
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ kernel cfg coverage OK (${_n} legs)${COLOR_RESET}"
}

# ── GATE-BLOB: the one artifact `check` has to build, and why it builds it at most once per tree ──
#
# TWO legs of the matrix compile `arch/aarch64/syscall.rs`: `arm-pi` (via `baremetal`) and
# `arm-tegra-el0` (via `tegra_el0`). That file `include_bytes!`s `target/user_blob.bin`, which is
# produced by `build_user_blob` — and `build_user_blob` runs only from `kernel8` and the ARMED
# `esp-jetson`, never from `check`. So `./arroyo check` in a tree that has never built a Pi image has
# been RED, on `arm-pi`, since the cfg matrix gained that leg:
#     error: couldn't read .../crates/kernel/../../target/user_blob.bin (os error 2)
#     error: could not compile `unaos-kernel` (lib) due to 1 previous error
# Reproduced on a `git worktree add`ed tree at f4454eff before this block existed. In an established
# tree a blob left over from some earlier `kernel8` masks it, which is why it reads as a recurring
# fresh-worktree papercut rather than a standing bug. It is also the reason `tegra_el0` had no leg:
# adding one on top of this would have doubled the papercut instead of closing it.
#
# ONLY WHEN MISSING — the load-bearing half. `build_user_blob` re-objcopies the file on every run, and
# `include_bytes!` registers it as a rebuild dependency, so calling it unconditionally from `check`
# would refresh the blob's mtime every single run and force both aarch64 EL0 legs to recompile from
# scratch each time, for a file whose CONTENTS `check` never reads. Existence is the entire
# requirement: `cargo check` neither links nor runs the blob, so a stale one type-checks identically.
# Present and non-empty => no-op, cost 0 (the warm case, i.e. always after the first time); absent =>
# build it once, ever. The media paths are unaffected: `kernel8` and armed `esp-jetson` call
# `build_user_blob` unconditionally, so neither ever consumes what this leaves behind.
#
# WHY NOT A STUB. Writing an empty/fake `user_blob.bin` would be cheaper still and is REJECTED,
# because the file then exists for every later command in that tree — including ones that bake it into
# media WITHOUT rebuilding it. `UNAOS_TEGRA_EL0=1 ./arroyo esp-arm` (likewise `arm` / `test-arm`)
# compiles `syscall.rs` and calls `build_user_blob` nowhere: today it fails loudly with the
# `couldn't read` above; with a stub on disk it would silently link a kernel whose EL0 payload is not a
# program, and the failure would move from the build to the bench. Masking a missing-artifact failure
# is exactly what must not happen, so `check` builds the REAL blob — same function, same assertions
# (`_start` = `mov x8,#1` = 28 00 80 d2) — or fails.
ensure_user_blob() {
    if [ -s "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/user_blob.bin" ]; then
        # BLOBATOMIC: re-run the entry-first assertion on the warm path too — a pre-atomic-era
        # truncated file (or any corruption) is rebuilt LOUDLY instead of accepted forever.
        local h4; h4="$(head -c 4 "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/user_blob.bin" | od -An -tx1 | tr -d ' \n')"
        if [ "$h4" = "280080d2" ]; then
            return 0
        fi
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}  target/user_blob.bin exists but fails the _start assertion (head4=${h4}) — rebuilding it${COLOR_RESET}"
    fi
    # Same TOOLBIN computation as `build_user_blob`'s other two callers — it requires the caller to
    # set it, and bash's dynamic scoping hands this `local` down into the call.
    local TOOLBIN; TOOLBIN="$(rustc +nightly --print sysroot)/lib/rustlib/$(rustc +nightly -vV | awk '/host/{print $2}')/bin"
    echo -e "${COLOR_CYAN}  (target/user_blob.bin absent — building it once, for the aarch64 EL0 legs)${COLOR_RESET}"
    build_user_blob
}

check_both() {
    # GATE-BLOB (above). BEFORE the first cargo invocation, not inside check_kernel_cfg: the DEFAULT
    # aarch64 leg below carries `tegra_el0` too whenever the operator's own knobs do
    # (`UNAOS_TEGRA_EL0=1 ./arroyo check`), so a blob supplied later would be supplied too late.
    ensure_user_blob
    echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚡ Syntax Check: x86_64...${COLOR_RESET}"
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR/crates/kernel"
    cargo +nightly check --release \
      --target ../../x86_64-unaos.json \
      $NIGHTLY_FLAGS $KERNEL_FEATURES
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ x86_64 OK${COLOR_RESET}"

    echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚡ Syntax Check: aarch64...${COLOR_RESET}"
    cargo +nightly check --release \
      --target ../../aarch64-unaos.json \
      $NIGHTLY_FLAGS $(arm_features)
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR"
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ aarch64 OK${COLOR_RESET}"

    # GATE-CFG: the cfg-gated coverage legs, immediately behind the two DEFAULT kernel legs so all the
    # kernel verdicts sit together in the scrollback. The default legs stay first and unchanged: they
    # are what the operator's own UNAOS_* knobs compile, and they are the fastest way to a diagnostic
    # for the edit that was just made. `_krc` carries a red leg past the userspace legs below, so a
    # green userspace can never mask a broken feature-gated kernel.
    #
    # TOOLFIX/BLOB: the `arm-pi` leg enables `baremetal`, which compiles
    # `arch/aarch64/syscall.rs` — and that module `include_bytes!`s `target/user_blob.bin`. The gate
    # therefore CONSUMES a build artifact that only the kernel8 path produces, so on a tree with an
    # empty `unaos/target/` (fresh clone/worktree, or post-`./arroyo clean`) `arm-pi` went red on a
    # missing file rather than on any source defect. Produce it here when it is absent so `check` is
    # self-contained. Existence-only on purpose: a warm tree pays nothing and the artifact's mtime,
    # which is the kernel crate's rebuild trigger, is never disturbed by a `check`.
    [ -f "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/user_blob.bin" ] || build_user_blob
    local _krc=0
    check_kernel_cfg || _krc=1

    # GATE-USER: the ring-3 crates, per arch, AFTER the kernel legs. Ordering is deliberate — the kernel
    # is what most edits touch, so its diagnostics stay at the top of the scrollback; and a userspace stub
    # that disagrees with the kernel is easier to read once the kernel's own verdict is already in.
    #
    # Both legs run even if the first fails, for the same reason check_user_arch does not stop at its own
    # first failure: one run should name every broken crate. `_urc` carries the verdict past the second
    # leg so a green aarch64 can never mask a red x86_64.
    local _urc=0
    check_user_arch x86_64  || _urc=1
    check_user_arch aarch64 || _urc=1

    # GATE-CORE: the HOST unit tests of the shared no_std cores the kernel links. `midden_core` is
    # the shell's one command table, parser and bare-name resolver (unaos/libs/sys/midden_core), and
    # its assertions are the only thing standing between a table edit and a shell that quietly
    # decides words differently — including the case rule, where `is_verb` disagreeing with the
    # case-insensitive FAT walk means a dropped `LS.ELF` can be launched by typing `LS`.
    #
    # WHY IT BELONGS IN `check` AND NOT IN A README. These ran hand-only, and a suite that is listed
    # as a gate in the docs but is not in any gate is exactly the vacuous instrument this tree keeps
    # catching. The cost is ~1 s: the crate is `no_std` + `alloc`, has zero dependencies, and builds
    # for the HOST here (the kernel legs above already proved it compiles for both targets).
    #
    # Root workspace, not `unaos/`: `midden_core` is a member of the repo-root `Cargo.toml`, the same
    # convention as `libs/fs/unafs` and `libs/sys/helm`, so `-p` is resolved from `WORKSPACE_DIR/..`.
    local _crc=0
    echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚡ Host tests: shared cores (midden_core)...${COLOR_RESET}"
    if (cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR/.." && cargo test -p midden_core); then
        echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ midden_core tests OK${COLOR_RESET}"
    else
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}  ❌ midden_core host tests FAILED${COLOR_RESET}"
        _crc=1
    fi

    if [ "$_urc" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}❌ check FAILED — userspace crates did not compile (see above)${COLOR_RESET}"
        return 1
    fi
    if [ "$_krc" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}❌ check FAILED — cfg-gated kernel code did not compile (see above)${COLOR_RESET}"
        return 1
    fi
    if [ "$_crc" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}❌ check FAILED — a shared core's host tests did not pass (see above)${COLOR_RESET}"
        return 1
    fi
}

build_both() {
    build_kernel_x86_64
    build_kernel_aarch64
}

# --- Real-hardware boot media (build + package an ESP, no QEMU) ---

# sha256 of a file on stdout — shasum (macOS) then sha256sum (Linux); empty if neither exists.
sha256_of() {
    if command -v shasum >/dev/null 2>&1; then shasum -a 256 "$1" | awk '{print $1}'
    elif command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1; then sha256sum "$1" | awk '{print $1}'
    else echo ""; fi
}

# SOURCE-ALONG: every piece of UnaOS boot media carries the source tree that built it.
#   (a) self-hosting rung 1 — the code travels with the machine that runs it;
#   (b) GPL-3.0-or-later source availability BY CONSTRUCTION — the corresponding source ships
#       on the same medium as the binary, so there is never a separate written-offer step.
# Packs `git archive HEAD | gzip` (TRACKED files only — no target/, no untracked junk) to
# <media>/SRC.TGZ plus <media>/SRC.SHA carrying the tarball's sha256 and the exact provenance
# (commit / branch / `git describe --always --dirty`). A dirty working tree is STAMPED -dirty but
# the archive is HEAD — committed state, never the dirt. ~6 MiB compressed today.
# Skippable with UNAOS_NOSRC=1 (tight media, CI speed); default ON for the esp-* media commands.
# See docs/dev/OS/10_INSTALL/source_along.md.
pack_source_along() {
    local dest="$1"
    if [ -n "${UNAOS_NOSRC:-}" ]; then
        rm -f "$dest/SRC.TGZ" "$dest/SRC.SHA" 2>/dev/null || true
        echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚡ SOURCE-ALONG skipped (UNAOS_NOSRC=1) — no SRC.TGZ on this media${COLOR_RESET}"
        return 0
    fi
    local root; root="$(git -C "$WORKSPACE_DIR" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || echo "")"
    if [ -z "$root" ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚠ SOURCE-ALONG: not a git checkout — no SRC.TGZ packed${COLOR_RESET}"
        return 0
    fi
    [ -d "$dest" ] || { echo -e "${COLOR_RED}ERROR: SOURCE-ALONG destination ${dest} does not exist${COLOR_RESET}"; exit 1; }
    echo -e "${COLOR_CYAN}⚡ SOURCE-ALONG: packing the source tree onto the media (SRC.TGZ)...${COLOR_RESET}"
    if ! git -C "$root" archive --format=tar HEAD | gzip -9 > "$dest/SRC.TGZ"; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}ERROR: git archive HEAD failed — SRC.TGZ not written${COLOR_RESET}"; exit 1
    fi
    [ -s "$dest/SRC.TGZ" ] || { echo -e "${COLOR_RED}ERROR: SRC.TGZ is empty${COLOR_RESET}"; exit 1; }
    local sha; sha="$(sha256_of "$dest/SRC.TGZ")"
    [ -n "$sha" ] || { echo -e "${COLOR_RED}ERROR: no shasum/sha256sum on PATH — cannot stamp SRC.SHA${COLOR_RESET}"; exit 1; }
    {
        echo "${sha}  SRC.TGZ"
        echo "commit   $(git -C "$root" rev-parse HEAD)"
        echo "branch   $(git -C "$root" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)"
        echo "describe $(git -C "$root" describe --always --dirty)"
    } > "$dest/SRC.SHA"
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ SRC.TGZ: $(wc -c < "$dest/SRC.TGZ" | tr -d ' ') bytes${COLOR_RESET}"
    echo -e "   sha256: ${sha}   (SRC.SHA beside it: sha + commit/branch/describe)"
}

# x86_64 boot media for the MacBook (UEFI). Emits TWO trees, because on x86 there are two volumes:
#
#   target/x86_64_esp/   the volume UEFI BOOTS  — EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI + kernel.elf (+ companions).
#                        Read once, by the bootloader, through firmware boot services. After
#                        ExitBootServices the kernel can never read it again.
#   target/x86_64_data/  the volume the KERNEL READS — HELLO.BIN / STAT.ELF / VUG.ELF / hello.txt.
#                        This is what `fs::fat::mount()` binds, which on x86 is ALWAYS the USB
#                        mass-storage device the xHCI driver enumerated, and nothing else (there is
#                        no SD/AHCI/NVMe driver on this arch, and BootInfo carries no boot-device
#                        handle, so the kernel cannot reach the ESP even in principle).
#
# WINX-7 PKG — WHY THIS SPLIT NOW. An attended rMBP boot reported
#   `:: WINX-2: STAT.ELF absent from the boot volume — end-to-end witness skipped ::`
# with STAT.ELF verifiably on the booted card: the firmware booted the SD-card ESP while xHCI
# enumerated a USB stick, so the shell's `bg /fat/STAT.ELF` searched the stick for a file the build
# had put on the card. The old one-line instruction ("copy the contents to a FAT32 USB root") was
# correct for a ONE-STICK boot and silently wrong for a two-device one; naming the two volumes is
# the fix. On a single stick, write BOTH trees to that one root and everything behaves as before.
esp_x86() {
    echo -e "${COLOR_CYAN}⚡ Building x86_64 kernel + bootloader and packaging the ESP (no QEMU)...${COLOR_RESET}"
    build_user_hello_x86   # U2: target/hello.bin — the builder copies it onto the ESP as HELLO.BIN
    build_user_stat_x86    # WINX-5: target/STAT-X86.ELF — staged as STAT.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    build_user_vug_x86     # WINX-7: target/VUG-X86.ELF — staged as VUG.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    build_user_pulse_x86   # PULSE-1: target/PULSE-X86.ELF — staged as PULSE.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR/builder"
    UNAOS_PACKAGE_ONLY=1 cargo +nightly run --release
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR"
    # The media path does NOT go through build_kernel_x86_64 (the builder compiles the kernel
    # itself), so the .rodata gate runs here too — on the STAGED image, which is the ELF the
    # firmware actually loads. A gate that only covers the QEMU path is not a gate on the boot media.
    gate_x86_rodata_no_code "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/kernel.elf"
    pack_source_along "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp"
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ x86 ESP: ${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/ (incl. HELLO.BIN for the U2 loader)${COLOR_RESET}"
    local _kelf="${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/kernel.elf"
    [ -f "$_kelf" ] && echo -e "   kernel.elf sha256: $(sha256_of "$_kelf")"
    [ -f "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/SRC.TGZ" ] && \
      echo -e "   SRC.TGZ    sha256: $(sha256_of "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/SRC.TGZ")"
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ x86 DATA volume: ${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_data/ (HELLO.BIN / STAT.ELF / VUG.ELF)${COLOR_RESET}"
    echo -e ""
    echo -e "   ${COLOR_CYAN}Two volumes, because the kernel cannot read the one it booted from:${COLOR_RESET}"
    echo -e "     BOOT volume  <- target/x86_64_esp/    (UEFI reads this; boot ⌥ Option → 'EFI Boot')"
    echo -e "     DATA volume  <- target/x86_64_data/   (the kernel's FAT mount = the USB stick on storage_slot)"
    echo -e "   ONE stick:  write BOTH trees to that stick's FAT32 root — they do not collide."
    echo -e "   TWO devices (e.g. SD-card ESP + USB stick): esp tree -> the bootable device,"
    echo -e "               data tree -> the USB stick, or \`bg /fat/VUG.ELF\` reports -ENOENT."
    echo -e "   Probe after boot: \`cat hello.txt\` must say 'DATA volume' — if it says anything else,"
    echo -e "               the kernel is reading a stick you did not stage."
}

# VMIMAGE-1: emit ONE self-contained, distributable bootable disk image — a GPT disk with a FAT32
# ESP carrying the SAME boot tree esp-x86 proves (EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI + kernel.elf + companions) +
# a README-VM.txt. Boots in stock QEMU+OVMF / UTM / VirtualBox/VMware with EFI on, with zero UnaOS
# tooling on the consumer's machine. The builder packages it in pure Rust (fatfs + hand-written GPT,
# builder/src/vm_image.rs); no host mkfs/hdiutil. Writes ONLY to target/vm/ (never disturbs the
# target/ state the test harness uses) and prints the image path + sha256.
vm_image() {
    # SOURCE-ALONG: the VM image's ESP is a FIXED 64 MiB FAT (vm_image.rs ESP_BYTES) built by
    # copying whatever sits in target/x86_64_esp — so a SRC.TGZ left there by an earlier esp-x86
    # would silently ride along and eat the budget. Media packaging for the VM image is out of this
    # arc's scope, so strip it and keep the image byte-comparable to the pre-SOURCE-ALONG one.
    rm -f "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/SRC.TGZ" "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/SRC.SHA" 2>/dev/null || true
    local git7; git7="$(cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR" && git rev-parse --short=7 HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo 0000000)"
    echo -e "${COLOR_CYAN}⚡ Building x86 boot products + packaging VM image (unaos-x86-${git7}.img)...${COLOR_RESET}"
    build_user_hello_x86   # U2: target/hello.bin — the builder copies it into the ESP as HELLO.BIN
    build_user_stat_x86    # WINX-5: target/STAT-X86.ELF — staged as STAT.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    build_user_vug_x86     # WINX-7: target/VUG-X86.ELF — staged as VUG.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    build_user_pulse_x86   # PULSE-1: target/PULSE-X86.ELF — staged as PULSE.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR/builder"
    UNAOS_VM_IMAGE=1 UNAOS_VM_GIT7="$git7" cargo +nightly run --release
    cd "$WORKSPACE_DIR"
    local img="${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/vm/unaos-x86-${git7}.img"
    if [ ! -f "$img" ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}ERROR: expected image ${img} was not produced${COLOR_RESET}"; exit 1
    fi
    # sha256: prefer shasum (macOS) then sha256sum (Linux); fail honestly if neither exists.
    local sha=""
    if command -v shasum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        sha="$(shasum -a 256 "$img" | awk '{print $1}')"
    elif command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        sha="$(sha256sum "$img" | awk '{print $1}')"
    else
        echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚠ neither shasum nor sha256sum found — skipping checksum${COLOR_RESET}"
    fi
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ VM image: ${img}${COLOR_RESET}"
    echo -e "   size: $(wc -c < "$img" | tr -d ' ') bytes"
    [ -n "$sha" ] && echo -e "   sha256: ${sha}"
    echo -e "   Boot it (stock QEMU+OVMF, no arroyo harness; disk attached over USB so the boot"
    echo -e "   log is saved back to UNAOS.LOG on the image — the kernel drives USB storage, not SATA):"
    echo -e "     qemu-system-x86_64 -machine q35 -m 1G \\"
    echo -e "       -drive if=pflash,format=raw,readonly=on,file=<OVMF_CODE.fd> \\"
    echo -e "       -drive if=none,id=unastick,format=raw,file=${img} \\"
    echo -e "       -device qemu-xhci -device usb-storage,drive=unastick,bootindex=0 -serial stdio"
    echo -e "   After shutdown, attach the .img and copy UNAOS.LOG off it (that is the boot log to"
    echo -e "   send back). README-VM.txt inside the image has the UTM / VirtualBox / VMware paths."
}

# Build the FAT32 test image(s) the kernel's read-only FAT reader parses (ls/cat). Ensures the x86
# ESP is packaged first (so EFI/ + kernel.elf populate the volume), then builds a partitioned image
# (MBR@LBA0 -> BPB) and a superfloppy (BPB@LBA0). Test with `UNAOS_FATIMG=1 ./arroyo test`.
fat_img() {
    # SOURCE-ALONG stays OFF for the FAT test fixtures: these are size-budgeted images (96 MiB FAT32,
    # 32 MiB FAT16) whose contents the kernel's read-only FAT tests enumerate — a multi-MiB SRC.TGZ
    # is neither wanted nor budgeted there. Also strip one left by a previous esp-x86 run.
    export UNAOS_NOSRC=1
    rm -f "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/SRC.TGZ" "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp/SRC.SHA" 2>/dev/null || true
    build_user_hello_x86   # U2: ensure target/hello.bin is fresh even if the ESP is reused below
    build_user_stat_x86    # WINX-5: target/STAT-X86.ELF — staged as STAT.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    build_user_vug_x86     # WINX-7: target/VUG-X86.ELF — staged as VUG.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    build_user_pulse_x86   # PULSE-1: target/PULSE-X86.ELF — staged as PULSE.ELF for `run`/`bg`
    if [ ! -d "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/target/x86_64_esp" ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_YELLOW}⚡ Packaging the x86 ESP first (payload for the FAT image)...${COLOR_RESET}"
        esp_x86
    fi
    echo -e "${COLOR_CYAN}⚡ Building FAT test images (MBR/GPT FAT32 + FAT16 + superfloppy)...${COLOR_RESET}"
    bash "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/scripts/make-fat-img.sh" part
    bash "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/scripts/make-fat-img.sh" gpt
    bash "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/scripts/make-fat-img.sh" p16
    bash "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/scripts/make-fat-img.sh" sf
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ FAT images: fat.img (MBR32), fat-gpt.img (GPT32), fat16.img (MBR16), fat-sf.img (superfloppy)${COLOR_RESET}"
    echo -e "   Test:  ./arroyo test-fat [part|gpt|p16|sf] 30"
}

# Headless FAT test the right way: package a FRESH ESP, rebuild the FAT image from it (so the
# usb-storage backing -- which OVMF may boot, since it carries EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI -- runs the
# SAME kernel under test, not a stale one), then boot with that FAT image as the block device.
# `./arroyo test-fat [part|sf] [secs]`. Mirrors a single-stick metal boot (boot + block are one FAT).
test_fat() {
    local layout="${1:-part}"
    local secs="${2:-30}"
    case "$layout" in part|gpt|p16|sf) ;; *) echo "usage: ./arroyo test-fat [part|gpt|p16|sf] [secs]"; exit 1;; esac
    export UNAOS_NOSRC=1   # SOURCE-ALONG off for the FAT test fixtures (see fat_img)
    esp_x86
    bash "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/scripts/make-fat-img.sh" "$layout"
    export UNAOS_FATIMG="$layout"
    test_x86_64 "$secs"
}

# SELFHOST-2: the on-shard source-verify gate. `./arroyo test-selfhost [layout] [secs]`.
#
# This is the ONE lane that packs SOURCE-ALONG onto a FAT fixture, and it must: `test-fat` sets
# UNAOS_NOSRC=1 precisely so its 96 MiB image stays enumerable byte for byte, so a gate that rode
# `test-fat` would boot a kernel looking for a SRC.TGZ the fixture had just been told not to carry --
# vacuous, and green while vacuous. Here SOURCE-ALONG is left ON for `esp_x86` and UNAOS_SRCFIXTURE=1
# tells make-fat-img.sh to stage the pair onto the image, so the kernel under test reads a REAL
# `git archive HEAD | gzip -9` of this very tree.
#
# The verdict is asserted HERE rather than left to the generic fault scan: a boot that never reached
# the witness at all would pass a scan for the absence of faults. `-> PASS ::` on the SELFHOST line is
# the positive claim, and the honest "nothing packed" line is failed explicitly so a silent
# UNAOS_NOSRC leak cannot read as success.
test_selfhost() {
    local layout="${1:-sf}"
    local secs="${2:-180}"
    case "$layout" in part|gpt|p16|sf) ;; *) echo "usage: ./arroyo test-selfhost [part|gpt|p16|sf] [secs]"; exit 1;; esac
    # RE-EXEC, and it is load-bearing. This script's knob->feature mapping (`_feats`, top of file) is
    # evaluated ONCE at script load, before dispatch, so a knob exported from inside a lane function
    # never reaches arroyo's OWN `cargo build`. That build runs LAST here (test_x86_64 rebuilds the
    # kernel after the ESP is packaged) and its output is the kernel that boots — so an in-function
    # export produced a builder banner reading `selfhost,...` over a booted kernel with the module
    # compiled OUT, and a gate that could only ever report "not packed". Re-exec with the environment
    # already set is the only way a lane can arm a knob for its own build.
    if [ -z "${UNAOS_SELFHOST:-}" ] || [ -n "${UNAOS_NOSRC:-}" ]; then
        exec env -u UNAOS_NOSRC UNAOS_SELFHOST=1 "$0" test-selfhost "$layout" "$secs"
    fi
    esp_x86
    UNAOS_SRCFIXTURE=1 bash "${WORKSPACE_DIR}/scripts/make-fat-img.sh" "$layout"
    export UNAOS_FATIMG="$layout"
    test_x86_64 "$secs" || true    # verdict is the witness line below, not the absence of faults
    local log="$SERIAL_LOG"
    echo -e ""
    if [ ! -s "$log" ]; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}❌ SELFHOST: ${log} is empty — the boot carries NO verdict${COLOR_RESET}"
        exit 4
    fi
    awk '/SELFHOST:/' "$log" || true
    if awk '/SELFHOST: src verified .* -> PASS ::/{f=1} END{exit !f}' "$log"; then
        echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ SELFHOST: the shard verified and enumerated its own source tree${COLOR_RESET}"
    else
        echo -e "${COLOR_RED}❌ SELFHOST: no 'src verified ... -> PASS ::' line in ${log}${COLOR_RESET}"
        exit 1
    fi
    scan_serial_faults "$log"
}

# aarch64 ESP for the Raspberry Pi 4 (UEFI / Path A). Copy onto a FAT32 SD card alongside the
# Tianocore RPi4 EDK2 firmware (RPI_EFI.fd + the Pi firmware blobs).
esp_arm() {
    prepare_aarch64
    pack_source_along "$ESP_DIR_ARM"
    echo -e "${COLOR_GREEN}✅ aarch64 ESP: ${ESP_DIR_ARM}${COLOR_RESET}"
    echo -e "   kernel.elf sha256: $(sha256_of "${ESP_DIR_ARM}/kernel.elf")"
    [ -f "${ESP_DIR_ARM}/SRC.TGZ" ] && echo -e "   SRC.TGZ    sha256: $(sha256_of "${ESP_DIR_ARM}/SRC.TGZ")"
